Description:

Attempts to create calendar events having the same UID in different calendar
resources now are rejected. Before, this already was enforced in CalDAV PUT,
but other APIs including JMAP Calendars did not enforce this rule. Existing
dav.db databases might contain entries that violate this rule. The
dav_reconstruct command now allows to repair such database.

Documentation:

docsrc/reference/manpages/usercommands/dav_reconstruct.rst

Config changes:

None.

Upgrade instructions:

Check existing dav.db database by running dav_reconstruct with the new
"--repair" argument, which reports data integrity errors but does not rewrite
the data. Use the additional "--really" argument to repair the data on disk.

Two limitations are worth knowing before running it:

- A copy that is split off under a new UID keeps its ORGANIZER and ATTENDEE
  properties.  Editing or destroying that copy afterwards schedules against a
  UID the recipients have never seen.  Cyrus has no per-resource scheduling
  suppression to prevent this.  The copy that carries a Schedule-Tag is
  preferred as the primary, so a copy with real scheduling state is only split
  off when every copy in the group has one.
- --repair alone is not a total dry run.  The index record rewrites that
  rebuilding the database performs -- assigning a fresh createdmodseq,
  expunging superseded records -- happen whether or not --really is given.

